  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nNext week continues to look warm and humid with limited chances for \nprecipitation...at least through Thursday.  Medium range guidance \nshows the relatively stagnant upper pattern persisting through the \nweek.  Long wave ridging over the western 1/2 of the CONUS and a \nlong wave trough over the east will keep the Mid Mississippi Valley \nin weak west to northwest flow aloft through the end of the week. \nThe upper level ridge tries to build east into the Mid Mississippi \nValley briefly on Monday and Tuesday, and as a result these will \nlikely be the two warmest days of the week with highs in the low to \nmid 90s.  Persistent low level southwest flow will keep dew point \ntemperatures high as well; consequently heat index values are \nforecast to rise to 100-108 across most of the area.  There is some \nuncertainty in the temperature forecast Monday and Tuesday as weak \nshort waves dip through the Midwest causing afternoon convection. \nLatest deterministic guidance shows these convective complexes \nmissing our area to the north both days, but a small shift in the \ntrack of the waves could produce convection farther south and lower \nafternoon temperatures.  The LREF acknowledges this possibility, and \nthe IQR for max temps Monday and Tuesday is 5-8 degrees, \nparticularly across northeast Missouri and west central Illinois \nwhich will be closer to wave track.  The short waves sharpen up the \nlong wave trough over the eastern U.S. by mid-late week.  The \ndeepening trough allows a cool surface high to build over the Great \nLakes which pushes a back-door cold front southwest into the Mid \nMississippi Valley.  Models vary on where the front will end up \nlater in the week, but the consensus is that the heat will break \nWednesday/Thursday as the front moves into the vicinity with \nincreasing chances for convection.",
